        "original_id": "55d847f4-3307-58ed-861d-15ad2e2b6f97",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Concepts: Blood and soil, Lebensraum\n- Organizations: Nazi Party, Nazi Germany\n- People: Friedrich Ratzel\n\nFacts:\n- Friedrich Ratzel was a German geographer and ethnographer who lived from August 30, 1844 to August 9, 1904.\n- Friedrich Ratzel was notable for first using the term Lebensraum in a sense later adopted by the National Socialists.\n- Blood and soil is a slogan expressing the nineteenth-century German idealization of a racially defined national body united with a settlement area.\n- The concept of Blood and soil is tied to the German idea of Lebensraum, which held that the German people needed to reclaim historically German areas of Eastern Europe for expansion.\n- The Nazi Party and Nazi Germany supported the most extreme form of the Lebensraum ideology until the end of World War II.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "34f8291a-869d-5c6c-b740-6adab0af4349",
        "text": "Page: Blood and Soil. Sentence: Blood and soil (German: \"Blut und Boden\" ) is a slogan expressing the nineteenth-century German idealization of a racially defined national body (\"blood\") united with a settlement area (\"soil\").\nPage: Blood and Soil. Sentence: The contemporary German concept Lebensraum, the belief that the German people needed to reclaim historically German areas of Eastern Europe into which they could expand, is tied to it.\nPage: Lebensraum. Sentence: The most extreme form of this ideology was supported by the Nazi Party (NSDAP) and Nazi Germany until the end of World War II.\nPage: Friedrich Ratzel. Sentence: Friedrich Ratzel (August 30, 1844 – August 9, 1904) was a German geographer and ethnographer, notable for first using the term \"Lebensraum\" (\"living space\") in the sense that the National Socialists later would.",
